(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-respiratory-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Respiratory Infections and Vaccines Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How long is psittacosis treated to prevent relapse?","choices":["3 days after fever resolves","5 to 7 days after fever resolves","10 to 14 days after fever resolves","21 days after fever resolves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An adequate course prevents relapse, commonly 10 to 14 days after the fever resolves. Doxycycline is started promptly, and clinical improvement often begins within 48 to 72 hours. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for Pneumocystis pneumonia?","choices":["Long-term steroid use","Cigarette smoking","Pet bird exposure","Cruise ship travel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pneumocystis pneumonia occurs with human immunodeficiency virus infection and other immunocompromise: chemotherapy, anti-rejection drugs, long-term steroids, organ transplant or blood cancer. It is the most common opportunistic infection in people with the virus. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"How many seasonal coronavirus disease 2019 vaccine doses do adults 65 or older generally need?","choices":["One dose","Two doses","Three doses","Four doses"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adults 65 or older generally need two seasonal doses about 6 months apart, while the updated vaccine is recommended for all adults 18 or older. Product, dose count and interval come from the current age and vaccination-history tables. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"How is psittacosis primarily acquired?","choices":["Soil with bird droppings","Circulating water droplets","Inhaled from sick birds","Person-to-person droplets"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Psittacosis, caused by the obligate intracellular bacterium Chlamydia psittaci, is acquired mainly through the respiratory system when handling sick birds, or by mouth-to-beak resuscitation. Human-to-human transmission is rare. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 81, 80"},{"q":"Which radiographic finding is characteristic of a lung abscess?","choices":["Diffuse bilateral perihilar infiltrates","Unilateral lower-lobe consolidation","Patchy interstitial infiltrates","Cavity with an air-fluid level"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A lung abscess appears as a cavitary lesion with an air-fluid level on the radiograph and on computed tomography. Its symptoms can be insidious and prolonged, with foul-smelling purulent sputum that can progress to hemoptysis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 54, 70, 86, 106"},{"q":"In Pneumocystis pneumonia associated with human immunodeficiency virus, which room-air arterial oxygen pressure indicates adjunctive corticosteroids?","choices":["Below 50 mm Hg","Below 60 mm Hg","Below 70 mm Hg","Below 80 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adjunctive corticosteroids are indicated when the room-air arterial oxygen pressure is below 70 mm Hg, started as early as possible, ideally within 72 hours of starting Pneumocystis therapy. Evidence outside the virus is less certain. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which influenza vaccine type should be avoided during pregnancy?","choices":["Recombinant vaccine","Inactivated vaccine","Any vaccine in the first trimester","Live attenuated vaccine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In pregnancy an age-appropriate inactivated or recombinant influenza vaccine is used in any trimester, and live attenuated vaccine is avoided. Live attenuated vaccine also has restrictions in immunocompromise. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Once cryptococcosis is diagnosed, which evaluation is often added to look for central nervous system or disseminated disease?","choices":["Lumbar puncture","Urine Histoplasma antigen","Serum beta-D-glucan","Bronchoalveolar lavage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cryptococcosis is diagnosed with serum cryptococcal antigen and respiratory culture or histology, then central nervous system or disseminated disease is evaluated, often with a lumbar puncture. Infectious disease consultation guides regimen and duration. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"How is mild acute histoplasmosis in an immunocompetent adult usually managed?","choices":["Observation without antifungals","Itraconazole for at least 12 months","Amphotericin-based induction","Fluconazole for 6 to 12 months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mild acute disease in an immunocompetent adult often needs observation rather than antifungals. Itraconazole is considered for prolonged or progressive illness or higher-risk hosts, and severe disease needs specialist-led amphotericin-based induction. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"Which influenza vaccine types are preferred for adults aged 65 or older?","choices":["Live attenuated vaccine","Egg-free standard-dose vaccine","Two standard doses 4 weeks apart","High-dose, adjuvanted or recombinant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adults 65 or older should preferentially receive a high-dose inactivated, adjuvanted inactivated or recombinant vaccine. If none of the preferred products is available, another age-appropriate vaccine is given rather than delaying vaccination. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"For a high-risk patient whose influenza symptoms began more than 48 hours ago, when is antiviral treatment started?","choices":["Promptly, even after 48 hours","Not given after 48 hours","After molecular test confirmation","If fever persists past 5 days"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Risk status supports prompt antiviral treatment even when symptoms began more than 48 hours ago. Oseltamivir is preferred for hospitalized, severe or progressive, and high-risk patients, including pregnancy, and is started promptly even after 48 hours. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 97, 108, 95"},{"q":"Which influenza test is preferred when the result will guide care?","choices":["A rapid molecular assay","A rapid antigen test","A viral culture","A chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Rapid molecular assays or reverse-transcription polymerase chain reaction are preferred over low-sensitivity antigen tests when feasible. Hospitalized patients are tested with a molecular assay, with multiplex influenza and coronavirus testing useful during co-circulation. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 104, 106"},{"q":"After one negative coronavirus antigen test in a symptomatic person, what is the next step?","choices":["Repeat the antigen test once after 7 days","Order chest imaging to identify the virus","Repeat in 48 hours, or amplification test","Accept it as ruling out the infection now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One negative antigen test does not exclude infection: repeat after 48 hours, for at least 2 tests if symptomatic (3 if asymptomatic), or obtain nucleic acid amplification, the most sensitive routine test. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"How is aspiration pneumonitis managed when bacterial infection is not suspected?","choices":["Prophylactic antibiotics","Routine anaerobic coverage","The pneumonia antibiotic regimen","Support of the airway and oxygenation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chemical pneumonitis begins with supportive care of oxygenation and the airway, and prophylactic antibiotics are avoided when bacterial infection is not suspected. Persistence or worsening raises concern for secondary infection, a complication or another diagnosis. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 51, 126"},{"q":"Which influenza antiviral should be avoided in a patient with asthma?","choices":["Baloxavir","Inhaled zanamivir","Oseltamivir","Intravenous peramivir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled zanamivir is avoided in asthma and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease because of bronchospasm risk. Oseltamivir is the preferred drug for high-risk patients. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"In acute bronchitis, what does green or yellow sputum indicate?","choices":["Progression to pneumonia","Pertussis infection","No clue to a viral or bacterial cause","A bacterial cause needing antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Colored mucus can occur in acute bronchitis but does not differentiate viral from bacterial infection. Purulent or colored sputum does not establish bacterial infection and does not justify antibiotics. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 16, 18"},{"q":"In an older adult, which factor most raises the risk of aspiration pneumonia?","choices":["Cruise ship travel","Pet bird exposure","Bat or bird droppings","Dysphagia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Risk factors for aspiration include dysphagia, altered consciousness and impaired airway protection; in the older patient, a swallowed bolus can enter the trachea. Swallowing, oral hygiene, positioning and reversible risks are assessed. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which vital-sign finding is common in psittacosis?","choices":["Tachycardia with hypotension","Hypothermia below 35 degrees","Marked hypertension","Relative bradycardia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Relative bradycardia is common in psittacosis, along with nonspecific auscultatory findings, splenomegaly in 10 to 70% and possible Horder spots (a macular facial rash resembling typhoid rose spots). — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 83, 27, 30"},{"q":"For an eligible adult who has never had a pneumococcal conjugate vaccine, which vaccine type completes the series with one dose?","choices":["A 20- or 21-valent conjugate vaccine","A 15-valent conjugate vaccine","A 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine","A 23-valent conjugate vaccine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One dose of the 20-valent or 21-valent pneumococcal conjugate vaccine completes the series for a conjugate-naive eligible adult. The 15-valent conjugate vaccine, by contrast, usually needs the 23-valent polysaccharide vaccine afterward. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 8, 9, 124"},{"q":"A Pneumonia Severity Index total of 95 points falls in which risk class?","choices":["Class II","Class III","Class IV","Class V"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. 91 to 130 points is class IV, with a mortality of 9.3%. The classes run 51 to 70 (class II, 0.6%), 71 to 90 (class III, 0.9%), 91 to 130 (class IV) and 131 to 395 (class V, 27.0%). — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which agent provides coverage for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus in pneumonia?","choices":["Ceftriaxone","Linezolid","Moxifloxacin","Ertapenem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus coverage is indicated, vancomycin or linezolid is used. It is added only for validated risk or local prevalence, and therapy is narrowed to culture and susceptibility results. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 40, 42, 46"},{"q":"Why are beta-lactam antibiotics ineffective against Mycoplasma pneumoniae?","choices":["It is a fungus","It is a virus","It lacks a cell wall","It produces beta-lactamase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-lactams are ineffective because Mycoplasma lacks a cell wall. Adults are treated with a macrolide or doxycycline, with a respiratory fluoroquinolone as an alternative; if a macrolide fails, reassess and consider doxycycline. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph pattern is classically caused by Streptococcus pneumoniae?","choices":["Bronchopneumonia","Interstitial pneumonia","Aspiration pattern","Lobar pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lobar pneumonia shows homogeneous consolidation, a silhouette sign, air bronchograms and a sharply marginated fissure, and its usual cause is Streptococcus pneumoniae. Each radiograph pattern has a usual cause. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"In a person with human immunodeficiency virus, below which CD4 (cluster of differentiation 4) count is primary Pneumocystis prophylaxis started whatever the viral load?","choices":["Below 50 cells/mm³","Below 100 cells/mm³","Below 200 cells/mm³","Below 350 cells/mm³"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary prophylaxis starts for a count below 100 cells/mm³, or for 100 to 200 when the virus is detectable in the blood. Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole is preferred, and prophylaxis stops only after guideline-defined immune recovery. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which cough treatment should be avoided routinely in uncomplicated acute bronchitis?","choices":["A short bronchodilator trial","Codeine-containing cough suppressants","Acetaminophen for discomfort","Rest, hydration and avoiding smoke"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Routine opioid cough suppressants are avoided: codeine-containing combinations have limited benefit and carry sedation, respiratory depression and misuse risks. Systemic or inhaled corticosteroids are also not routinely prescribed. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which treatment is avoided in coronavirus disease 2019 without hypoxemia, unless another indication exists?","choices":["Supportive home care","Systemic corticosteroids","Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir","Remdesivir"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic steroids are not used for coronavirus disease 2019 without hypoxemia unless another indication exists. Dexamethasone is reserved for hospitalized adults with hypoxemia; oxygenation and severity, not a positive test, set the pathway. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 120, 122"},{"q":"What is antigenic shift in influenza A?","choices":["Resistance to adamantane drugs","Reassortment of genes from 2 strains","Gradual surface protein mutations","Spread between people by droplet nuclei"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Antigenic shift is a major change in which genes from 2 strains reassort during coinfection of a single host, creating novel strains that challenge human immunity and may cause more severe outbreaks. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 91, 93, 94, 109"},{"q":"How is hospital-acquired pneumonia defined?","choices":["Onset more than 48 hours after intubation","Present early in the admission","Onset in a nursing-home resident","Onset 48 hours or more after admission"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hospital-acquired pneumonia develops 48 hours or more after admission and was not incubating on admission. Ventilator-associated pneumonia develops more than 48 hours after endotracheal intubation. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"In the CURB-65 (confusion, urea, respiratory rate, blood pressure, age 65 or older) score, which respiratory rate earns a point?","choices":["20 or more per minute","24 or more per minute","30 or more per minute","35 or more per minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A respiratory rate of 30 or more breaths per minute scores 1 point, as do confusion, urea above 20 mg/dL (7 mmol/L), systolic pressure below 90 or diastolic 60 or less, and age 65 or older. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slides 31, 29"},{"q":"Which organism is the usual cause of bronchopneumonia that progresses to multilobar cavitation and abscess?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Pneumocystis jirovecii","Mycoplasma pneumoniae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bronchopneumonia, poorly defined patchy infiltrates scattered through the lung with air bronchograms, is usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus, which can progress to multilobar involvement, cavitation, pneumatoceles and abscess. — 37. Respiratory Infections - Bobilin - 10-29-2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
